## Details

### Arguments

| Attribute | | |
|---|---|---|
| `installer` | required | The installer image by digest, `<prefix>/<name>-installer@sha256:<64 hex>` (lower-case hex, no tag beside the digest), as `stowage ship` prints it. An installer carries one release, so the digest is the release. `<name>` is the project's name: `describe` must report the same, or the create is refused. |
| `inputs` | optional map | Non-secret inputs, each given as `--set KEY=VALUE`. They are on the target's command line and in state. A key is a letter or `_`, then letters, digits or `_`; `tls` is refused (the answers file's reserved section). An input given a non-empty value here that `describe` marks `secret` (its `inputs`, and the inputs a TLS mode adds, `tls_inputs`: the secret `STOWAGE_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` of `dns-cloudflare`, from Stowage 0.38.0) is refused: at create, and at an update (every key of the map, changed or not) for the running release and the one an upgrade goes to, before anything runs. A key taken out of the map is given empty (`--set KEY=`) to the re-run: the installer's rule for an empty value decides (a required input is refused, an advanced one goes back to its default); a key taken out that `secret_inputs_wo` now holds is not (`--set` would win over its answer), and, given another value than `inputs` gave, needs a new `secret_inputs_wo_version` in the same apply (else a plan error, `an input moved to secret_inputs_wo`, on every target; OpenTofu plans again with the final configuration before an apply, so an apply never sees one). A refresh reads the value the install records (`status --json --values`) for each key given and keeps it in the resource's private state: one whose record changed since the last record (the refresh before, or the end of the last create or update, which reads it too) and differs from the configuration's (a kit taken gave it, a re-run by hand) is put in state, so the next plan gives the configuration's again; a value the installer records otherwise than given (an empty one as its default) records the same each time, so it is no change; a file input is compared by its digest. |
| `secret_inputs_wo` | optional map, **write-only** | Secret inputs, never in plan or state, sent on standard input as an answers file (`--answers`). A key in both maps is refused. |
| `secret_inputs_wo_version` | optional number | Change it to apply a change of `secret_inputs_wo` (a write-only value is never compared): the update re-sends every secret input the running release declares, a TLS mode's (`STOWAGE_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN`) included; without a change an upgrade sends only those the new release adds. Leaving it out with `secret_inputs_wo` set is a warning. |
| `secret_rotations` | optional map of numbers | A number per generated secret of the release (`{ INTERNAL_TOKEN = 1 }`), as `random_password`'s `keepers`: a changed number rotates that secret in that apply, by state's installer fetched into a private directory on this machine as for the other runs — its three passes `<short>-stow k8s secrets rotate KEY --stage`, the swap and `--finish` (`--namespace`, `--release`, `--kube-context` as the release's), each restarting the workloads that read it, for a secret whose spec has `rotation: true`; one with `replace: true` rotates in one pass on Docker and the VM alone (`describe` says `none` on Kubernetes), so it is refused here. A key's first number — at the create, or a key added later — is recorded and rotates nothing; a key taken out is forgotten. A key the release does not rotate on this target (`describe --json` `secrets[].rotate`: `none`, or no way named for the target), or that is no generated secret of it, is refused (`KEY does not rotate on SHAPE`, `not a generated secret of the release`; a way this provider does not run: `KEY rotates in a way this provider does not run`): at the create, before the install; at plan, when the plan keeps `installer` and the provider read the `describe` of the release state runs (at the create, a rotation, or, while `secret_rotations` is set, an update after which the ways kept are not the running release's — a read that fails then is a warning, `… (the release's rotate ways not read)` —, kept in the resource's private state; not after a refresh that reads another release, nor for a state made by an earlier provider or an import never applied with `secret_rotations`); else by the apply, after the update's other steps (saved) and before any rotation. A release whose `describe` lists no `secrets` (one built by Stowage before 0.46.0) takes none (`secret_rotations needs a newer release`). The keys rotate in key order, after the update's other steps, on the release the install runs then, within the update's timeout; the first that fails stops the rest, which keep their number (the next plan shows them again), and a rotation by passes resumes in the next apply at the pass that failed (`The rotation of KEY stopped at …`; running a pass again is safe: one whose change the installer recorded brings the stack up with it), the passes before not run again. A resumed pass that fails again keeps the resume point (`Rotating KEY stopped part-way`); when the target's rotation was finished or moved on by hand since, take the key out, apply, then give it back: the apply that adds it records its number and forgets where the rotation stopped, so its next rotation starts at `--stage`. A first pass refused changed nothing and leaves no resume point, and a key whose way is no longer `passes` (another release) drops its own; a stopped rotation whose number is given back to state's is a plan warning (`the rotation of KEY is not finished`). |
| `given_wo` | optional, **write-only** | What another install gives (the installer's `--given` YAML), used at create, on standard input. |
| `registry_login_wo` | optional, **write-only** | The install's own registry login: a Docker config holding one `auths` entry for the host of the release's registry or of the provider's `registry` (`{"auths": {"HOST": {"auth": "BASE64(USER:PASSWORD)"}}}`), minted for this install in the registry (revoking it leaves every other install pulling). Never on a command line or in state: a file on standard input, in a private directory removed afterwards. Sent at create and when `registry_login_wo_version` changes (a version given without it is refused at validation, `registry_login_wo_version without registry_login_wo`; an empty one is an apply error, `registry_login_wo is not given`); left out with its version, the install keeps the login it holds. The installer refuses a login for another host, or one that does not read, before anything is written. `k8s install --registry-login-file` (the release's pull Secret written from it, so the nodes pull with it); when the version changes, the release applied again with it (`k8s upgrade --registry-login-file`, alone or with the re-run or the upgrade); with `image_pull_login` refused at validation (`registry_login_wo with image_pull_login`); beside a rollback, `a rollback goes alone` (helm's rollback writes no Secret). |
| `registry_login_wo_version` | optional number | Change it to send `registry_login_wo` again. `registry_login_wo` without it is a warning; it without `registry_login_wo` (null) is refused at validation (`registry_login_wo_version without registry_login_wo`). A refresh that reads the install holding no login (`status --json --values` `pull_login` false, `pull_login_error` empty) takes it out of state, so the next plan sends the login again. |
| `kit_wo` | optional, **write-only** | A handoff kit, never in plan or state: the ephemeral `stowage_kit`'s `kit`, issued in the same apply (see [handoff kits](../guides/handoff-kits.md)), or a kit made by hand (`file("x.kit")`), given to the first install (`--kit`, a file on standard input) with `kit_code_wo` (`--kit-code-file`, on standard input). Without its code at create, an apply error before anything runs (`kit_code_wo is not given`). First-install only; a kit for an install that exists is `stowage_kit_take`'s. With `given_wo`, refused at validation (`kit_wo with given_wo`). |
| `kit_code_wo` | optional, **write-only** | The kit's code (the ephemeral `stowage_kit`'s `code`), never on a command line or in state, sent at create alone; without `kit_wo` or `kit_wo_version`, refused at validation (`kit_code_wo without kit_wo`). It may be left out once the install took the kit. |
| `kit_wo_version` | optional string | Says a kit is given at create: `stowage_kit`'s `id`, or any value for a kit made by hand. With it, a create whose `kit_wo` is null (the ephemeral `stowage_kit` is null outside the apply that issued the kit, so an apply that failed after issuing it lost it) is an apply error before the install runs (`The kit is not here`, naming the fix: bump the `stowage_kit`'s `version`, which issues a kit again in the same apply), never an install without the kit; a create finding the release installed (a retry after a create that failed once the install was done) gives no kit and needs none, the take-over's warning naming `kit_wo`. A create retried while the release is still installing (helm's `pending-install`) is refused by the take-over's own refusal of a busy release, before the kit is asked for: apply again once it ends. A later change runs nothing on the target, with a plan warning (`kit_wo is first-install only`, said too when it is not known yet because its `stowage_kit` issues a kit in this apply: the install takes nothing unless the plan replaces it), and is recorded. With `given_wo`, refused at validation (`kit_wo with given_wo`). |
| `allow_rollback` | default `false` | Lets a change of `installer` to `previous.installer` run the kept installer's `rollback`. Without it, that change is a plan error (and an apply error when `status --json` names it `previous` only at apply); while it is not known yet (a value from another resource), the apply decides, `a rollback goes alone` included. |
| `deletion_protection` | default `true` | While true, a destroy is refused (`deletion_protection is on`). Set it `false` and apply first. |
| `delete_data` | default `false` | A destroy runs `uninstall --data` (the volumes deleted) instead of `uninstall` (kept). |
| `plan_dry_run` | default `false` | On Docker, a plan changing `installer` alone (no re-run of the running release beside it) runs the change's dry run on the target, the step chosen from the install's `status --json` by the apply's own rule (its errors — `a rollback needs allow_rollback`, `installer is another digest of the release installed`, first-install settings the install holds otherwise — at plan, or the apply's alone), so the installer's refusal is a plan error (its reason the summary, its fix the detail): an upgrade's — `sh -c '…' sh <work_dir>` removes the plans' directories older than an hour there, then makes a private (0700) `<work_dir>/.stowage-plan.XXXXXX` beside the install; the new installer is fetched into it (as at create, logged in the same way) and described, the inputs checked as the apply checks them (a secret input in `inputs` refused before anything runs), then `sh ./<name>.sh upgrade --dry-run --no-input --json --dir <work_dir>/<name> [--set K=V]… [--ports]` runs in that directory (what it extracts stays there) with what the upgrade would be given (the inputs, secret inputs as `--answers` and ports the new release adds), and the directory is removed (only a `.stowage-plan.XXXXXX` of the work directory is; a removal that fails is a warning); a rollback's — the inputs checked as for the upgrade (a secret input in `inputs`, and on this machine an input the run's environment sets, refused before anything runs), then the kept installer's `rollback --dry-run --json`, without the inputs' variables as the apply's. A plan that also re-runs the running release gets a warning instead (`No dry run of this plan`: the upgrade runs on what that re-run leaves; told before anything is fetched when the change is the running release's whatever the new one is: TLS, domains, a secret input, an input it declares that the plan gives, a port of a route it has), a rollback with one is `a rollback goes alone` (as at apply, its status's `previous` deciding a rollback); an install gone since its status was read gets a warning too (the apply says what it finds); an input moved to `secret_inputs_wo` without a new version is refused before anything is fetched; one whose configuration holds a value not known yet (but `deletion_protection`, `delete_data` and `timeouts`, which no dry run reads), or with a setting a run reads not known yet (the pull account, `registry`, `registry_ca`, the mirror's login, `public_keys`), no dry run, with a warning (`No dry run of this plan yet`: OpenTofu plans again at apply, with it known, and that plan runs it); an install whose home `work_dir` was never resolved (an import's), nothing but a warning (`Taking over the install: its values are not read at plan`).  A failure is a plan error too. The fetched image stays in the daemon's store, and the dry run takes the install's lock while it reads it (an apply of the same install meanwhile is refused by it). Opt-in: the plan reaches the target, and OpenTofu plans again at apply, which runs it again. On an imported state never applied it runs too, and says what the take-over changes (`Taking over the install: …`); its rollback is refused (`a rollback of an imported install`). On Kubernetes and the VM: see their guides (the new installer's `k8s upgrade --dry-run` or `k8s rollback --dry-run`; the VM's `upgrade --version V --dry-run` or `rollback --dry-run` over SSH, Stowage 0.38.0 or later on its OS). |
| `timeouts` | block | `create` (default 30m; it bounds the wait for a first install still `installing`), `read` (5m), `update` (30m), `delete` (15m). A run cut by its timeout is a failure. |

A null value in `inputs`, `secret_inputs_wo`, `domains` or `ports` is refused by its key
(`<attribute>.<key> is null`); a value not known until apply (an ephemeral variable's, one computed
from another resource) is not, its key checked when the configuration is validated.

### The release

| Attribute | | |
|---|---|---|
| `namespace` | required | The release's namespace (`--namespace`), a DNS label (lower-case letters, digits and `-`, at most 63 characters; else refused). First-install only. |
| `release` | optional | The helm release's name (`--release`), a DNS label of at most 53 characters; left out, the project's name, the installer's default. First-install only (the effective name is compared: naming the default changes nothing). |
| `image_pull_login` | optional bool | Writes the cluster's pull Secret for the mirror from the provider's pull account (`k8s install|upgrade --image-pull-login`), at every install and upgrade: the run gets a Docker configuration of its own (a private temporary directory, removed afterwards) holding `username`'s login to the host of the registry the release pulls from (the one state records, else the provider's `registry`), the password on standard input, never an argument. Opt-in, as the account's credential is then readable by whoever reads the release's Secrets. Turned on alone for an installed release, the update applies the release again (`k8s upgrade --no-input --json --image-pull-login`) to write it; with a rollback, it is refused (`a rollback goes alone`: helm's rollback writes no Secret); turned off, nothing runs and the Secret stays. A plan with it and no `username`, or no provider `registry` (and none recorded), is an error (`image_pull_login needs the pull account`, `… a mirror`); the installer refuses the release's own registry, and a login the nodes cannot pull with. |
| `values` | optional | A chart values file, YAML whose document is a mapping (`yamlencode({...})`; else refused at validation: `values is not a chart values file`), given to helm after the installer's own values (`k8s install|upgrade --values`, the file on standard input, never an argument) at the install, every upgrade and every re-run of the release; helm's rollback takes back the values of the release it reaches, so after a rollback the release is applied again with them by the installer of the release it reached (`k8s upgrade --no-input --json --values`; fetched before anything changes; that re-apply failing leaves `values` null in state, so the next plan gives them; `plan_dry_run` dry-runs the rollback, not that re-apply). Each such re-apply, as a change of `values` alone, is a helm revision of the same release: `previous` may then name the release that runs, so the release before it is no longer a rollback's (as after a change of `inputs` alone). Changed alone for an installed release, the update applies the release again (`k8s upgrade --no-input --json --values`); with a rollback, it is refused (`a rollback goes alone`: helm's rollback takes back the values of the release it reaches). helm keeps the values each run gave (`--reset-then-reuse-values`): a key left out keeps its earlier value, and a key given `null` drops it (the chart's default again). Left out, nothing runs and the release keeps its values, with a plan warning (`values left out: the release keeps its values`). An imported release's first apply gives them again, as they are not read (its plan, with `plan_dry_run`, says so: `… applied again for values`). Kept in state in the clear: give a secret as a secret input, or in a Secret of your own the chart reads. |

### Attributes read from the target

| Attribute | |
|---|---|
| `id` | `<namespace>/<release>`. |
| `short_name` | The installer's short name: a Docker install keeps its installer as `<id>/<short_name>-stow` (`describe`'s at create, then `status --json` `short_name` at each read and update, so a rollback across a release that renamed it finds the one kept); a VM runs its OS bundle's `<short_name>-stow`. |
| `registry_ca_digest` | `sha256:<hex>` of the registry CA the install keeps (`status --json` `registry_ca`; on Kubernetes, the provider's at the install), null for none. On Docker and the VM a plan whose provider `registry_ca` has another digest (none included) is an error (`the provider's registry_ca is first-install only`), but for an imported state never applied. An imported state holds none until its first apply, which records it (planned as known after apply); on a VM whose status reports none, the provider's. |
| `version`, `revision` | The release installed (`status --json` `installed`). |
| `channel` | The channel the install follows. |
| `status` | `installed`, `installing` (the first install has not finished; helm's `pending-install` on Kubernetes), `failed: <why>` (`install_failed` on Docker; a latest helm revision neither deployed nor pending on Kubernetes), `pending: <why>` (Kubernetes: helm working on the release), or `not installed`. |
| `previous` | `{version, revision, installer}`, the release `rollback` returns to; null before an upgrade. |
| `registry` | The provider's `registry` at the install; null in an imported state until its first apply. |

### Plan-time errors

A change of a first-install-only setting — `namespace`, the helm release (`release`, else the
project's name), the provider's `registry`, or the project an `installer` names (an imported
release's is checked at the first apply) — is a plan error naming the old value, never a
replacement: replacing an install deletes its data.

A key of `secret_rotations` added or changed that the release does not rotate on this target is a plan error when the provider read the release's ways before and the plan keeps the release (see `secret_rotations`).

Also plan errors: `installer` changed to `previous.installer` without `allow_rollback` (`a
rollback needs allow_rollback`); a rollback with any other change of what the release applies
(`a rollback goes alone`); `image_pull_login` without the provider's pull account or a mirror.
A plan changing anything the release applies plans `status`, `version`, `revision`, `channel`
and `previous` unknown (the update reads them after its run); one changing `installer` also
`short_name`. An imported state's first apply also plans `registry` and `registry_ca_digest`
unknown (it records them).

